distribution for the first draft token, the second draft token conditioned on the first, and so on.\n\nThe key trick is verification. The target model accepts draft tokens only when they are consistent with its own distribution. A common algorithm samples from the target distribution while using the draft as a proposal: if the target probability for a token is at least the draft probability, the token is accepted; otherwise it is accepted with probability p_target/p_draft and, if rejected, a replacement token is sampled from a residual distribution. This rejection-sampling construction guarantees that the final token sequence is distributed exactly as if the target model had decoded alone, so quality and output distribution are unchanged.\n\nIn practice, speedup depends on how many draft tokens are accepted before the first rejection, the relative cost of the draft and target models, and the batch size. If the draft is highly correlated with the target, many tokens are accepted and the target\u2019s expensive forward pass produces several tokens at once. If the draft is weak, the target often rejects early, and the extra draft cost may not pay off.\n\nThe two most common reasons the speedup is smaller than hoped are low draft-target agreement, especially on difficult or out-of-domain text, and high target-model cost or large batch sizes, which reduce the benefit of verifying multiple tokens in one pass.\n\nCount words. Let's count manually.\nSpeculative1 decoding2 accelerates3 large4 language5 model6 inference", "top2": [[[1596, -0.005657613277435303], [1421, -5.928379535675049]], [[1144, -8.940736734075472e-06], [513, -11.939106941223145]], [[4087, -0.25481176376342773], [310, -1.5371699333190918]], [[1156, -0.001518746605142951], [303, -6.651112079620361]], [[579, -0.20971141755580902], [25, -2.051159381866455]], [[1622, -0.0006455478142015636], [3296, -8.151406288146973]], [[25, -0.4885745346546173], [13, -0.9981361031532288]], [[59987, -0.07577819377183914], [78